  <div class="post-layout">
    <article class="post-body">
      <a href="/en/index.html#practice" class="back-link">← Back to Services</a>

      <!-- SCOPE SWITCHER -->
      <div class="scope-switcher">
        <div class="scope-tab active">
          <div class="scope-tab-label">Part I</div>
          <div class="scope-tab-title">Foreign Arbitral Awards</div>
          <div class="scope-tab-text">New York Convention · PILPL Arts. 60–62 · ICC, HKIAC, UNCITRAL, LCIA, ISTAC awards</div>
        </div>
        <div class="scope-tab">
          <div class="scope-tab-label">Part II</div>
          <div class="scope-tab-title">Foreign Court Judgments</div>
          <div class="scope-tab-text">PILPL Arts. 50–59 · Bilateral treaty enforcement · Reciprocity requirement</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="key-takeaways">
        <div class="key-takeaways-head"><span>Key Takeaways</span></div>
        <div class="kt-grid">
          <div class="kt-item">
            <div class="kt-num">01</div>
            <div class="kt-label">Exequatur is mandatory</div>
            <p class="kt-text">Every foreign arbitral award or court judgment requires a court enforcement order (exequatur) before it can be executed against assets in Turkey — regardless of institution or country of origin.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="kt-item">
            <div class="kt-num">02</div>
            <div class="kt-label">No merits review</div>
            <p class="kt-text">Turkish courts cannot re-examine whether the tribunal or foreign court reached the correct conclusion. Review is limited to procedural compliance and public policy.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="kt-item">
            <div class="kt-num">03</div>
            <div class="kt-label">Grounds for refusal are exhaustive</div>
            <p class="kt-text">Only the grounds listed in the New York Convention Art. V (for awards) or PILPL Art. 54 (for judgments) can block enforcement. Public policy is the most frequently invoked — and the most narrowly interpreted.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="intro-box">
        Winning an international arbitration or obtaining a foreign court judgment is only part of the process. Where the debtor holds assets in Turkey, the award or judgment must be <strong>recognised and enforced by Turkish courts</strong> before it can produce binding legal consequences. Without this judicial approval — known as <strong>exequatur</strong> — neither an arbitral award nor a foreign court judgment can be executed in Turkey, regardless of where it was rendered.
      </div>

      <!-- LEGAL FRAMEWORK — TWO PARALLEL TRACKS -->
      <section id="legal-framework">
        <h2>Legal Framework — Two Parallel Tracks</h2>
        <p>Turkey maintains two distinct enforcement regimes: one for foreign arbitral awards and one for foreign court judgments. Understanding which track applies is the first — and most consequential — step in any cross-border enforcement strategy.</p>
        <p>Foreign arbitral awards benefit from the <strong>1958 New York Convention</strong> on the Recognition and Enforcement of Foreign Arbitral Awards, to which Turkey has been a party since 1992. This provides a multilateral, relatively uniform framework for enforcement across more than 170 contracting states.</p>
        <p>Foreign court judgments, by contrast, have no equivalent multilateral treaty. Their enforcement in Turkey is governed exclusively by the <strong>International Private and Procedural Law (PILPL), Law No. 5718, Articles 50–59</strong>, and depends on the existence of a bilateral treaty or de facto reciprocity between Turkey and the country where the judgment was issued.</p>
        <div class="takeaway">
          ICSID awards occupy a separate category entirely. Under the Washington Convention on the Settlement of Investment Disputes Between States and Nationals of Other States — ratified by Turkey in 1988 — ICSID awards are enforceable as of right in all member states without the requirement of domestic court merits review. This makes ICSID the most direct enforcement route where investment treaty claims are involved.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- PART I: ARBITRAL AWARDS -->
      <section id="framework">
        <h2>Part I — Enforcing Foreign Arbitral Awards in Turkey</h2>
        <p>Two primary sources of law govern the enforcement of foreign arbitral awards in Turkey.</p>

        <h3>The New York Convention (1958)</h3>
        <p>Turkey ratified the Convention on the Recognition and Enforcement of Foreign Arbitral Awards in 1992, <strong>with a reciprocity reservation</strong>. This means Turkey will enforce awards rendered in other New York Convention signatory states — a network covering over 170 countries. The Convention establishes a strong presumption in favour of enforcement and limits the grounds on which courts may refuse recognition to those exhaustively listed in Article V.</p>
        <p>Turkey also declared a <strong>commercial reservation</strong>, meaning the Convention applies only to disputes arising from commercial — as opposed to purely civil — legal relationships. This distinction is relevant where the underlying contract or transaction could be characterised as non-commercial under Turkish law.</p>

        <h3>PILPL — Law No. 5718 (Arts. 60–62)</h3>
        <p>For awards from non-signatory states, or where bilateral treaty provisions apply, enforcement is governed by Turkey's <strong>Private International Law and Procedural Law (PILPL)</strong>, specifically Articles 60–62. The PILPL applies a parallel but not identical set of conditions — and in certain respects is more restrictive than the Convention, including an additional requirement of reciprocity between Turkey and the country of origin.</p>
        <p>Where the New York Convention applies, it takes precedence as the primary instrument. The PILPL fills procedural gaps relating to documentation, court jurisdiction, and the conduct of exequatur proceedings.</p>

        <div class="takeaway">
          Turkey's New York Convention ratification includes both a reciprocity reservation and a commercial reservation. Awards from non-signatory states must be pursued under the PILPL — which carries additional requirements, including proof of reciprocity. Always verify the applicable legal basis before filing.
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="process">
        <h2>The Exequatur Process — Step by Step</h2>
        <p>Enforcement requires filing an <strong>exequatur lawsuit</strong> before the competent Turkish courts. There is no shortcut: even awards from the ICC, HKIAC, UNCITRAL, LCIA, or ISTAC require this process. ICSID awards are the exception — discussed below.</p>

        <div class="protection-grid">
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="pcard-icon">01</div>
            <div>
              <div class="pcard-title">Identify the Competent Court</div>
              <p class="pcard-text">Applications are filed before the Civil Courts of First Instance (Asliye Hukuk Mahkemesi) at the domicile of the award debtor in Turkey — or, if no Turkish domicile exists, at the location of the assets to be attached.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="pcard-icon">02</div>
            <div>
              <div class="pcard-title">Prepare the Required Documents</div>
              <p class="pcard-text">The applicant must submit the original or certified copy of the arbitral award, the original or certified copy of the arbitration agreement, and notarised Turkish translations of both documents prepared by a sworn translator certified by the Turkish Ministry of Justice.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="pcard-icon">03</div>
            <div>
              <div class="pcard-title">File the Exequatur Petition</div>
              <p class="pcard-text">The petition sets out the legal basis (New York Convention or PILPL), confirms that the conditions for enforcement are satisfied, and requests the court to issue an enforcement order. The petition must be filed by a Turkish-admitted attorney.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="pcard-icon">04</div>
            <div>
              <div class="pcard-title">Court Review — No Merits Examination</div>
              <p class="pcard-text">Turkish courts do not review the merits of the underlying dispute (révision au fond is prohibited). The court examines only whether the conditions for enforcement are met. The award debtor is served and may raise objections based on the enumerated grounds.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="pcard-icon">05</div>
            <div>
              <div class="pcard-title">Enforcement Order Issued</div>
              <p class="pcard-text">If the conditions are satisfied and no refusal grounds are established, the court issues an exequatur. The foreign award then has the same legal effect as a domestic Turkish court judgment.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="pcard-icon">06</div>
            <div>
              <div class="pcard-title">Execution Proceedings</div>
              <p class="pcard-text">The creditor initiates execution proceedings before the relevant Enforcement Office (İcra Müdürlüğü) to attach and liquidate the debtor's assets in Turkey.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="refusal">
        <h2>Grounds for Refusing Enforcement — New York Convention Art. V</h2>
        <p>Under both the New York Convention and the PILPL, Turkish courts may only refuse enforcement on specific, exhaustive grounds. No other basis for refusal is available — and the burden of proof on most grounds rests with the party resisting enforcement.</p>

        <h3>Grounds the debtor must raise — Article V(1)</h3>
        <div class="refusal-grid">
          <div class="refusal-cell header">Ground</div>
          <div class="refusal-cell header">What it means in practice</div>
          <div class="refusal-cell"><span class="ground">Invalid arbitration agreement</span><span class="detail">The agreement is not valid under the applicable law, or the parties lacked capacity at the time of signing.</span></div>
          <div class="refusal-cell"><span class="ground">Due process violation</span><span class="detail">The debtor was not given proper notice of the proceedings, or was otherwise unable to present their case.</span></div>
          <div class="refusal-cell"><span class="ground">Award exceeds submission</span><span class="detail">The award deals with matters outside the scope of the arbitration agreement or the parties' submission.</span></div>
          <div class="refusal-cell"><span class="ground">Irregular tribunal or procedure</span><span class="detail">Tribunal composition or procedure was not in accordance with the parties' agreement or the law of the seat.</span></div>
          <div class="refusal-cell"><span class="ground">Award not binding or set aside</span><span class="detail">The award has not yet become binding on the parties, or has been annulled or suspended at the seat of arbitration.</span></div>
        </div>

        <h3>Grounds the court raises ex officio — Article V(2)</h3>
        <div class="refusal-grid">
          <div class="refusal-cell header">Ground</div>
          <div class="refusal-cell header">What it means in practice</div>
          <div class="refusal-cell"><span class="ground">Non-arbitrability</span><span class="detail">The subject matter of the dispute is not capable of settlement by arbitration under Turkish law.</span></div>
          <div class="refusal-cell"><span class="ground">Public policy (ordre public)</span><span class="detail">Recognition or enforcement of the award would be contrary to Turkish public policy. Most frequently invoked ground — and most narrowly interpreted by Yargıtay.</span></div>
        </div>

        <div class="pullquote">
          Turkish courts cannot re-examine whether the tribunal reached the correct legal or factual conclusion. The public policy assessment is limited to whether enforcing the award would be manifestly incompatible with fundamental Turkish legal principles.
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- PART II: FOREIGN JUDGMENTS -->
      <section id="judgments">
        <h2>Part II — Enforcing Foreign Court Judgments in Turkey</h2>
        <p>Foreign court judgments — as distinct from arbitral awards — are enforced in Turkey under <strong>PILPL Articles 50–59</strong>. There is no multilateral convention equivalent to the New York Convention for court judgments, so enforcement depends on bilateral treaty or de facto reciprocity between Turkey and the country of origin.</p>

        <h3>Conditions for enforcement under PILPL Art. 54</h3>
        <div class="protection-grid">
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="pcard-icon">A</div>
            <div>
              <div class="pcard-title">Finality</div>
              <p class="pcard-text">The foreign judgment must be final and enforceable in the country where it was rendered. A judgment subject to appeal or not yet final cannot be enforced in Turkey.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="pcard-icon">B</div>
            <div>
              <div class="pcard-title">Reciprocity</div>
              <p class="pcard-text">There must be legal or de facto reciprocity between Turkey and the country of origin — either via bilateral treaty or established practice of mutual enforcement. This is a threshold requirement with no New York Convention equivalent for judgments.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="pcard-icon">C</div>
            <div>
              <div class="pcard-title">Jurisdictional basis</div>
              <p class="pcard-text">The foreign court must have had proper jurisdiction over the matter. Turkish courts will examine whether the foreign court's jurisdiction is compatible with Turkish private international law rules.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="pcard-icon">D</div>
            <div>
              <div class="pcard-title">Due process compliance</div>
              <p class="pcard-text">The defendant must have been duly served and given an adequate opportunity to participate in the foreign proceedings.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="pcard-icon">E</div>
            <div>
              <div class="pcard-title">No conflicting Turkish judgment</div>
              <p class="pcard-text">The foreign judgment must not conflict with a final Turkish court judgment on the same matter between the same parties.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="pcard">
            <div class="pcard-icon">F</div>
            <div>
              <div class="pcard-title">Public policy</div>
              <p class="pcard-text">Enforcement must not violate Turkish public policy (ordre public). As with arbitral awards, this ground is interpreted narrowly — general disagreement with the foreign court's reasoning does not suffice.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="takeaway">
          The reciprocity requirement is the most distinctive feature of the foreign judgment enforcement regime — and the most variable. For arbitral awards, the New York Convention eliminates this uncertainty for 170+ states. For court judgments, reciprocity must be assessed case by case. Counsel should verify the current state of Turkey's enforcement practice with the relevant country before filing.
        </div>

        <h3>Country-Specific Reciprocity Status</h3>
        <p>The table below reflects general practice based on available Turkish court precedent and treaty instruments. Country-specific assessment is always recommended before filing.</p>
        <div class="reciprocity-grid">
          <div class="rec-cell header">Country of Origin</div>
          <div class="rec-cell header">Reciprocity Status</div>
          <div class="rec-cell header">Basis</div>

          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="country">United Kingdom</span></div>
          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="rec-status-tag recognized">Generally Recognised</span></div>
          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="basis">Turkish court precedent on mutual enforcement practice (de facto)</span></div>

          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="country">Germany</span></div>
          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="rec-status-tag recognized">Generally Recognised</span></div>
          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="basis">Bilateral treaty and established de facto practice</span></div>

          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="country">United States</span></div>
          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="rec-status-tag variable">Variable — Case by Case</span></div>
          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="basis">No bilateral treaty; de facto assessment conducted per U.S. state</span></div>

          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="country">United Arab Emirates</span></div>
          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="rec-status-tag recognized">Generally Recognised</span></div>
          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="basis">Judicial cooperation agreement between Turkey and UAE</span></div>

          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="country">China</span></div>
          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="rec-status-tag limited">Not Established</span></div>
          <div class="rec-cell"><span class="basis">No bilateral treaty; no consistent de facto enforcement practice</span></div>
        </div>

        <h3>Arbitral awards vs. foreign judgments — a comparison</h3>
        <div class="compare-grid">
          <div class="compare-col">
            <div class="compare-head">Foreign Arbitral Awards</div>
            <div class="compare-body">
              <ul>
                <li>Governed by New York Convention (170+ states) + PILPL Arts. 60–62</li>
                <li>Reciprocity presumed for Convention signatory states</li>
                <li>Grounds for refusal exhaustively listed in Art. V</li>
                <li>No merits review permitted</li>
                <li>ICSID awards enforceable as of right — no domestic merits review</li>
                <li>BIT-based awards may benefit from additional treaty protections</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="compare-col">
            <div class="compare-head">Foreign Court Judgments</div>
            <div class="compare-body">
              <ul>
                <li>Governed by PILPL Arts. 50–59 — no multilateral convention</li>
                <li>Reciprocity must be established affirmatively (treaty or de facto)</li>
                <li>Jurisdictional review is an additional condition</li>
                <li>No merits review permitted</li>
                <li>Subject to conflict with existing Turkish judgments</li>
                <li>More variable — enforcement prospects depend on origin country</li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="practical">
        <h2>Practical Considerations for Creditors</h2>

        <ul class="checklist">
          <li>
            <strong>Identify and preserve assets early</strong>
            Enforcement is only as valuable as the assets available to attach. A precautionary attachment (ihtiyati haciz) before or alongside the exequatur application may be appropriate if there is any risk the debtor may dissipate assets. This can be obtained within days of application.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Translation quality is not a formality</strong>
            Turkish courts have rejected enforcement applications due to translation deficiencies. Documents should be translated by a sworn translator certified by the Turkish Ministry of Justice.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>The arbitration clause determines enforcement prospects</strong>
            A defective arbitration agreement is one of the most common grounds for resisting enforcement of arbitral awards. Clauses should clearly specify the institution, seat, language, and governing law.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Monitor set-aside proceedings at the seat</strong>
            If the award debtor has commenced annulment proceedings at the seat of arbitration, Turkish courts may stay the enforcement proceedings pending the outcome.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>ICSID awards operate differently</strong>
            For ICSID awards, Turkey's obligations under the ICSID Convention (ratified 1988) provide a separate and more direct enforcement route. ICSID awards are enforceable as of right in all member states without domestic court review of the merits.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Turkey-qualified counsel is required</strong>
            The exequatur petition must be filed by an attorney admitted to a Turkish bar. Involving Turkey-qualified counsel during the arbitration — rather than only at the post-award stage — allows for more effective coordination on enforcement strategy.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            Can a foreign arbitral award be enforced in Turkey without a lawsuit?
            <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            No. An <strong>exequatur lawsuit</strong> must be filed before a competent Civil Court of First Instance (Asliye Hukuk Mahkemesi). There is no administrative or expedited route — even awards from major institutions such as the ICC, LCIA, or HKIAC must go through this process. ICSID awards are the exception.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            Can a foreign court judgment (not an arbitral award) be enforced in Turkey?
            <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            Yes. Foreign court judgments are enforceable in Turkey under PILPL Articles 50–59, subject to conditions including: finality of the judgment, <strong>reciprocity</strong> between Turkey and the country of origin, due process compliance, no conflicting Turkish judgment, and consistency with Turkish public policy. Unlike arbitral awards, there is no multilateral convention framework — enforcement depends on bilateral treaty or de facto reciprocity.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            Do Turkish courts review the merits of the foreign award or judgment?
            <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            No. Turkish courts are expressly prohibited from conducting a <em>révision au fond</em> for both arbitral awards and foreign court judgments. The court's jurisdiction in exequatur proceedings is strictly limited to verifying whether the conditions for enforcement are satisfied and whether any listed grounds for refusal apply.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            How long does enforcement take in Turkey?
            <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            A standard exequatur proceeding typically takes between <strong>6 and 18 months</strong>, depending on court workload and the complexity of objections raised. Where there is a risk the debtor may dissipate assets, a <strong>precautionary attachment (ihtiyati haciz)</strong> can be sought independently — often within days — to secure assets pending the main enforcement order.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            What is the most common ground for refusal in Turkish courts?
            <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            The <strong>public policy (ordre public)</strong> exception is the most frequently invoked ground for both arbitral awards and foreign judgments. However, the Turkish Court of Cassation (Yargıtay) has consistently interpreted this exception narrowly: only a violation of <em>fundamental</em> Turkish legal principles can block enforcement. General disagreement with the outcome does not satisfy this threshold.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-question" aria-expanded="false">
            What documents are required to file an exequatur application?
            <span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            For arbitral awards: (1) the original or certified copy of the award; (2) the original or certified copy of the arbitration agreement; and (3) notarised Turkish translations of both, by a sworn translator certified by the Turkish Ministry of Justice. For foreign judgments: the original or certified copy of the final judgment and a notarised Turkish translation. Translation quality has been a basis for rejection in Turkish courts and should not be treated as a formality.
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="conclusion">
        <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
        <p>Turkey's enforcement framework is well-established and, under the New York Convention, generally creditor-friendly for arbitral awards. Foreign court judgments face a more variable landscape — reciprocity is the critical threshold, and its availability depends on the country of origin.</p>
        <p>For both award and judgment creditors, the most effective enforcement strategies are built at the outset — not after an award or judgment is issued. Proper arbitration clause drafting, early asset identification, and the involvement of Turkey-qualified counsel are decisions that materially affect what happens after the proceedings conclude.</p>
